<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-product-rule" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Product Rule</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>The rule for differentiating a product of functions is slightly more complicated than the previous rules.</p>

    <example>
      <title>The Derivative of a Product Is Not the Product of the Derivatives</title>
      <p>One might at first glance think that the derivative of a product of two functions should be the product of the derivatives. Especially because the derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives (sum rule). Unfortunately, this is not true. In other words,</p>
      <me>\frac{d}{dx} \brac{f(x) g(x)} \neq f'(x) \cdot g'(x)</me>
      <p>For example, if <m>f(x)=x^2</m> and <m>g(x)=x^3</m>, the derivative of the product is,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} \brac{f(x) g(x)} \amp= \frac{d}{dx} \brac{x^2 \cdot x^3}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \frac{d}{dx} x^5</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= 5x^4</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>However, <m>f'(x) = 2x</m> and <m>g'(x) = 3x^2</m>, so <m>f'(x) \cdot g'(x) = 6x^3</m>, which is not the same as <m>5x^4</m>.</p>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-product-rule">
      <title>Product Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <me>\boxed{(fg)' = f'g + fg'} \quad \text{"$fg$ prime equals $f$ prime times $g$ plus $f$ times $g$ prime"}</me>
        <p>Or, with the function arguments,</p>
        <me>\brac{f(x) \cdot g(x)}' = f'(x)g(x) + f(x)g'(x)</me>
        <p>Or,</p>
        <me>\frac{d}{dx} \brac{f(x) g(x)} = \brac{\frac{d}{dx}f(x)} \cdot g(x) + f(x) \cdot \brac{\frac{d}{dx}g(x)}</me>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>Intuitively, if we call <m>f</m> the 1st function and <m>g</m> the 2nd function, then it says the derivative of their product is,</p>
    <me>\boxed{\text{"Derivative of the 1st"} \times \text{"2nd"} + \text{"1st"} \times \text{"Derivative of the 2nd"}}</me>
    <p>It's like writing the product twice, and each time you take the derivative of exactly one of the functions.</p>

    <remark>
      <p>Note that because the two terms are added, their order doesn't matter. So <m>(fg)' = f'g + fg' = fg' + f'g</m>.</p>
    </remark>

    <example>
      <title>Simple Product (Expanding Also Works)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = (2x+1)(x^2-3)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>This is the product of <m>2x+1</m> and <m>x^2-3</m>, so the product rule says,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \underbrace{2}_{\text{deriv of 1st}} \cdot \underbrace{(x^2-3)}_{\text{2nd}} + \underbrace{(2x+1)}_{\text{1st}} \cdot \underbrace{2x}_{\text{deriv of 2nd}}</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Then, we can expand and simplify,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(x) \amp= 2x^2 - 6 + 4x^2 + 2x</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 6x^2 + 2x - 6</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>In this case, you may recognize that we could have expanded the function first, and then differentiated term by term,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f(x) \amp= (2x+1)(x^2-3)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 2x^3 + x^2 - 6x - 3</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Then,</p>
        <me>f'(x) = 6x^2 + 2x - 6</me>
        <p>Both methods give the same answer. Here, expanding first is a bit simpler, because the expansion isn't too challenging.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>More Complicated Product</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = (x^2+3x+1)(x^3-2x+4)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>In this case, expanding is more complicated, so the product rule would work better.</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \underbrace{(2x+3)}_{\text{deriv of 1st}} \cdot (x^3-2x+4) + (x^2+3x+1) \cdot \underbrace{(3x^2-2)}_{\text{deriv of 2nd}}</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>This is a valid final answer. If we need the derivative at a specific point, say <m>x = 1</m>, we can plug in directly without expanding,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(1) \amp= (2+3)(1-2+4) + (1+3+1)(3-2)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 5(3) + 5(1) = 20</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>This is much faster than trying to expand everything first.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Product with Radicals</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Differentiate <m>f(x) = (x - \sqrt[3]{x})(\sqrt{x}+2)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Expanding is a bit complicated here, because there are 2 radicals. Instead, we can use the product rule,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \underbrace{\brac{1 - \frac{1}{3}x^{-2/3}}}_{\text{deriv of 1st}} \cdot (\sqrt{x}+2) + (x - \sqrt[3]{x}) \cdot \underbrace{\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}}_{\substack{\text{deriv of} \\ \text{2nd}}}</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>This is a valid final answer, however, if you wanted to plug in a specific value of <m>x</m>, it is helpful to rewrite the exponents as positive,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \brac{1 - \frac{1}{3x^{2/3}}} \cdot (\sqrt{x}+2) + (x - \sqrt[3]{x}) \cdot \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Note that <m>x^{-1/2} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}</m> and <m>x^{-2/3} = \frac{1}{x^{2/3}}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>In general,</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>Expand first</alert> if it's simple to do, and would make the function simpler.</p></li>
      <li><p><alert>Use the product rule</alert> if the expansion is complicated and would take a long time.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Later on, we'll see that there are many situations where you can't expand.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-product-rule-examples">
    <title>Examples</title>

    <project>
      <title>Product Rule (No Simplification)</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative, using the product rule. No need to simplify your answer.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (2x-1)(x^2+1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 2(x^2+1)+(2x-1)(2x)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = x(3x-8)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 1\cdot(3x-8)+x\cdot 3</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (x^3+x^2+1)(x^2+2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = (3x^2+2x)(x^2+2)+(x^3+x^2+1)(2x)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = x^2(1+x-3x^2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 2x(1+x-3x^2)+x^2(1-6x)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (x^4+x^2-1)(x^2-2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = (4x^3+2x)(x^2-2)+(x^4+x^2-1)(2x)</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Product Rule Two Ways</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative in two ways: using the product rule, and by expanding first. Verify that both answers are equal, by simplifying.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (x+1)(x^2-x+1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (3x+4)(x-1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 6x + 1</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (2x+3)(5x^2-4x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 30x^2+14x-12</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = x(x-1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 2x - 1</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (3-x^2)(x^3-x+1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = -5x^4+12x^2-2x-3</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=(1+2x^2)(x-x^2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=1-2x+6x^2-8x^3</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (x^3+4x^2+x)(x-1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 4x^3 + 9x^2 - 6x - 1</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Evaluate at a Point</title>
      <introduction><p>Evaluate each derivative at the given point.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>y=(2+7x)(x-3)</m>, <m>x=2</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>9</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y=(1-2x)(1+2x)</m>, <m>x=\frac{1}{2}</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>-4</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=(5x^3+7x^2+3)(2x^2+x+6)</m>, find <m>f'(-1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(-1)=-8</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y=(3-2x-x^2)(x^2+x-2)</m>, <m>x=-2</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>-9</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (1-2x)(3x-4)</m>, <m>x = 2</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>-13</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (1-x+x^2)(x-2)</m>, <m>x = 1</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>0</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = x^4(4x^3+2)</m>, <m>x = -1</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>20</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Tangent Lines</title>
      <introduction><p>Find the equation of the tangent line to the given curve at the given point.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>y=(x^3-5x+2)(3x^2-2x)</m> at <m>(1,-2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y=-10x+8</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (2-\sqrt{x})(1+\sqrt{x}+3x)</m> at <m>(1,5)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y = x + 4</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Differentiate and Simplify</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative and simplify.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=(x^2-3x)(x^5+2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=7x^6-18x^5+4x-6</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=x(x-4)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=2x-4</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=x^2(2x-1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=6x^2-2x</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=(3x+2)(2x-7)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=12x-17</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=(5x^7+1)(x^2-2x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=45x^8-80x^7+2x-2</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=(x^2+1)(3-2x^2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=-8x^3+2x</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>\frac{d}{dx}((x+1)(3x+2))</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>6x + 5</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = x^3(x^2+2x+3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = x^2(5x^2+8x+9)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (1-x^2)(2-x^3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 5x^4-3x^2-4x</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (3x^3+4)(1-2x^3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = -36x^5-15x^2</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = 3x^4(2x^2-1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 12x^3(3x^2-1)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (x-1)(x^3+x^2+x+1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 4x^3</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (5x^7+5x)(6x^3+3x^2+3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 300x^9 + 135x^8 + 105x^6 + 120x^3 + 45x^2 + 15</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Differentiate and Simplify (Radicals)</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative and simplify.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>\frac{d}{dx}(x^2(2\sqrt{x} + 1))</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>5x^{3/2} + 2x</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{x}(1-x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{3x^{2/3}} - \frac{4}{3}x^{1/3}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}(x-2\sqrt{x}+2)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x} - 2 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (x-x^2)\brac{2x-x^{4/3}}</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 4x - 6x^2 - \frac{7}{3}x^{4/3} + \frac{10}{3}x^{7/3}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}(2-x^2+5x^4)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}} - \frac{5}{2}x^{3/2} + \frac{45}{2}x^{7/2}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (x-\sqrt{x})(x^2+\sqrt{x})</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 3x^2 - \frac{5}{2}x^{3/2} + \frac{3}{2}\sqrt{x} - 1</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Differentiate and Simplify (More Radicals)</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative and simplify.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (2-\sqrt{x})(1+\sqrt{x}+3x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = \frac{1+10\sqrt{x}-9x}{2\sqrt{x}}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (2\sqrt{x}-1)(4x+1)^{-1}</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = \frac{-4x+4\sqrt{x}+1}{\sqrt{x}(4x+1)^2}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (2-3\sqrt{x})(4-\sqrt{x})</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 3 - \frac{7}{\sqrt{x}}</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Differentiate with Constants</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative and simplify.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (ax+b)(cx^2-d)</m>, where <m>a, b, c, d</m> are constants</p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 3acx^2+2bcx-ad</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x)=\sqrt{x}(a+bx)</m>, where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are constants</p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=\frac{a+3bx}{2\sqrt{x}}</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <project>
      <title>Negative Exponents</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = \brac{\frac{1}{x^2}-\frac{3}{x^4}}(x+5x^3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=5+\frac{14}{x^2}+\frac{9}{x^4}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (x^3-2x)(x^{-4}+x^{-2})</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y'=1+\frac{1}{x^2}+\frac{6}{x^4}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = x^{-2}(x^3-3x^2+6)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 1 - 12x^{-3}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (6+x^{-2})(8x^{10}-5x^3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = -2x^{-3}(8x^{10}-5x^3)+(6+x^{-2})(80x^9-15x^2)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = \brac{1+\frac{1}{x^2}}(x^2+1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 2x - \frac{2}{x^3}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (x^2+1)\brac{x+5+\frac{1}{x}}</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 3x^2+10x+2-\frac{1}{x^2}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (1+x^2)(x^{3/4}-x^{-3})</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = \frac{11}{4}x^{7/4}+\frac{3}{4}x^{-1/4}+x^{-2}+3x^{-4}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x^2} - \frac{3}{x^4}</m>. Hint: Rewrite as <m>x^{-2} - 3x^{-4}</m> and use power rule.</p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x)=-2x^{-3}+12x^{-5}</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = x^2(2+x^{-3})</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 4x - \frac{1}{x^2}</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <exercise>
      <title>Slope with Negative Exponents</title>
      <statement><p>Find the slope of <m>y = x^{-5}(1+x^{-1})</m> at <m>x=1</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>-11</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Derivative Two Ways</title>
      <statement><p>If <m>f(x) = (6x^4-3x^2+1)(2-x^3)</m>, find <m>f'(1)</m> two ways: (a) by using the Product Rule, and (b) by expanding <m>f(x)</m> first.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>f'(1) = 6</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <project>
      <title>Derivative at a Point</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative at the given point.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (1+x-2x^2)(3x^3+x-1)</m>, <m>x = 1</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>-9</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = (2-3\sqrt{x})(4-\sqrt{x})</m>, <m>x = 4</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>-\frac{1}{2}</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <exercise>
      <title>Horizontal Tangent (Linear Factors)</title>
      <statement><p>Find the points where the tangent to <m>y=2(x-29)(x+1)</m> is horizontal.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>(14,-450)</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Horizontal Tangent (Repeated Factor)</title>
      <statement><p>Find the points where the tangent to <m>y=(x^2+2x+1)(x^2+2x+1)</m> is horizontal.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>(-1,0)</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-extended-product-rule">
    <title>Extended Product Rule (3 or More Factors)</title>

    <p>The product rule can be extended to 3 functions multiplied together. It turns out that,</p>
    <me>\boxed{(fgh)' = f' gh + fg' h + fgh'}</me>
    <p>Observe the pattern: there are 3 terms, and in each term, exactly one of the functions is differentiated, and the other two are left unchanged.</p>

    <remark>
      <p>This rule comes from using the product rule twice. First, we can think of <m>fgh</m> as <m>(fg)h</m> (the function <m>fg</m> together, multiplied by <m>h</m>). Then, applying the product rule to <m>(fg)h</m>, we get,</p>
      <me>(fgh)' = (fg)' \cdot h + (fg) \cdot h'</me>
      <p>Then, for <m>(fg)'</m>, we can apply the product rule again, to get,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\amp= (f'g + fg') \cdot h + (fg) \cdot h'</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= f'gh + fg'h + fgh' \amp\amp \text{distributing}</mrow>
      </md>
    </remark>

    <project>
      <title>Three-Factor Product Rule</title>
      <introduction><p>Find each derivative, using the product rule for 3 functions, and simplify.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(x) = (x+1)(x+2)(x+3)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>f'(x) = 3x^2+12x+11</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = \sqrt{x}(3x+5)(6x^2-5x+1)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 63x^{5/2} + \frac{75}{2}x^{3/2} - 33\sqrt{x} + \frac{5}{2\sqrt{x}}</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <exercise>
      <title>Three-Factor Derivative at a Point</title>
      <statement><p>Find the derivative of <m>y=x(5x-2)(5x+2)</m> at <m>x=3</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>671</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-product-rule-given-values">
    <title>Product Rule with Given Function Values</title>

    <project>
      <title>Given Function Values</title>
      <introduction><p>Find the indicated derivative using the given function values.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(5) = 1</m>, <m>f'(5) = 6</m>, <m>g(5) = -3</m>, <m>g'(5) = 2</m>. Find <m>(fg)'(5)</m>.</p></statement><hint><p><m>(fg)'(5) = f'(5)g(5) + f(5)g'(5) = 6(-3) + 1(2)</m></p></hint><answer><p><m>-16</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(4) = 2</m>, <m>g(4) = 5</m>, <m>f'(4) = 6</m>, <m>g'(4) = -3</m>. Find <m>(fg)'(4)</m>.</p></statement><hint><p><m>f'(4)g(4) + f(4)g'(4) = 6(5) + 2(-3)</m></p></hint><answer><p><m>24</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(1) = 5</m>, <m>f'(1) = 4</m>, <m>g(1) = 2</m>, <m>g'(1) = 3</m>. Find <m>\frac{d}{dx}(f(x)g(x))\big|_{x=1}</m>.</p></statement><hint><p><m>f'(1)g(1) + f(1)g'(1) = 4(2)+5(3)</m></p></hint><answer><p><m>23</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(3) = 2</m>, <m>f'(3) = 5</m>, <m>g(3) = 2</m>, <m>g'(3) = -10</m>. Find <m>p'(3)</m> where <m>p(x) = f(x)g(x)</m>.</p></statement><hint><p><m>f'(3)g(3)+f(3)g'(3) = 5(2)+2(-10)</m></p></hint><answer><p><m>-10</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>f(2) = 3</m>, <m>f'(2) = 5</m>, <m>g(2) = -1</m>, <m>g'(2) = -4</m>. Find <m>(fg)'(2)</m>.</p></statement><hint><p><m>(fg)'(2) = f'(2)g(2)+f(2)g'(2) = 5(-1)+3(-4)</m></p></hint><answer><p><m>-17</m></p></answer></task>
    </project>

    <exercise>
      <title>Tangent Line from Given Values</title>
      <statement><p>Find the equation of the tangent line to the graph of <m>F(x)=f(x) g(x)</m> at <m>x=1</m>, given that <m>f(1) = 2</m>, <m>f'(1) = -3</m>, <m>g(1) = 4</m>, <m>g'(1) = -2</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>F(1) = 8</m>, <m>F'(1) = (-3)(4)+(2)(-2) = -16</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>y = -16x + 24</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-product-rule-advanced">
    <title>Advanced Examples</title>

    <exercise>
      <title>Derivative with Unknown Function</title>
      <introduction><p>If <m>f</m> is a differentiable function, find an expression for the derivative of each of the following functions.</p></introduction>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = xf(x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = f(x) + xf'(x)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = x^2 f(x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 2xf(x) + x^2 f'(x)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = \sqrt{x}f(x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = \frac{f(x)}{2\sqrt{x}}+\sqrt{x}f'(x)</m></p></answer></task>
      <task><statement><p><m>y = x^c f(x)</m></p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = cx^{c-1}f(x)+x^c f'(x)</m></p></answer></task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Slope of xf(x)</title>
      <statement><p>Find the slope of the tangent line to <m>y = xf(x)</m> at <m>(1,2)</m>, given <m>f(1) = 2</m>, <m>f'(1) = 2</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>y' = f(x)+xf'(x)</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>4</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Tangent to xf(x)</title>
      <statement><p>Given that <m>f(3) = 4</m>, <m>f'(3) = -2</m>, and <m>g(x) = xf(x)</m>. Find the tangent to <m>g</m> at <m>x = 3</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>g(3) = 12</m>, <m>g'(3) = 4 + 3(-2) = -2</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>y = -2x + 18</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Tangent to x²f(x)</title>
      <statement><p>Given that <m>f(2) = 2</m>, <m>f'(2) = 3</m>, <m>g(x) = x^2 f(x)</m>. Find the tangent to <m>g</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>g(2) = 8</m>, <m>g'(2) = 4(2) + 4(3) = 20</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>y = 20x - 32</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Tangent Using Given Tangent</title>
      <statement><p>The tangent to <m>h(x)</m> at <m>x = 4</m> is <m>y = -3x + 14</m>. Find the tangent to <m>y = (x^2-3x)h(x)</m> at <m>x = 4</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>h(4) = 2</m>, <m>h'(4) = -3</m>; <m>y'(4) = 5(2)+4(-3) = -2</m>, point <m>(4,8)</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>y = -2x+16</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Second Derivative</title>
      <statement><p>Given that <m>f(2) = 10</m> and <m>f'(x) = x^2 f(x)</m> for all <m>x</m>, find <m>f''(2)</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>f''(x) = 2xf(x) + x^2 f'(x)</m>, so <m>f''(2) = 4(10) + 4(40)</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>200</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Tangent from Two Tangent Lines</title>
      <statement><p>The tangent to <m>f</m> at <m>x = 2</m> is <m>y = 4x+1</m> and the tangent to <m>g</m> at <m>x = 2</m> is <m>y = 3x-2</m>. Find the tangent to <m>y = f(x)g(x)</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p><m>f(2) = 9</m>, <m>f'(2) = 4</m>, <m>g(2) = 4</m>, <m>g'(2) = 3</m>; <m>y'(2) = 43</m>, point <m>(2, 36)</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>y = 43x - 50</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Derivative of [f(x)]²</title>
      <task><statement><p>Show that <m>\frac{d}{dx}[f(x)]^2 = 2f(x)f'(x)</m>, using the product rule with <m>g=f</m>.</p></statement></task>
      <task><statement><p>Use part (a) to differentiate <m>y = (2+5x-x^3)^2</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>y' = 2(2+5x-x^3)(5-3x^2)</m></p></answer></task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>Derivative of [f(x)]³</title>
      <statement><p>Show that <m>\frac{d}{dx}[f(x)]^3 = 3[f(x)]^2 f'(x)</m>, using the product rule for 3 functions.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>(fff)' = f'ff+ff'f+fff' = 3f^2 f'</m></p></answer>
    </exercise>

    <remark>
      <p>In fact, the pattern for the derivative of a product of 3 functions continues for any number of functions. For example, for 4 functions, say, <m>f, g, h, k</m>, we have,</p>
      <me>(fghk)' = f'ghk + fg'hk + fgh'k + fghk'</me>
      <p>In general, for <m>n</m> functions, say, <m>f_1(x), f_2(x), \dots, f_n(x)</m>,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} \brac{f_1(x) f_2(x) \cdots f_n(x)} \amp= f_1'(x)f_2(x)\cdots f_n(x) + f_1(x)f_2'(x)\cdots f_n(x) + \cdots + f_1(x)f_2(x)\cdots f_n'(x)</mrow>
      </md>
    </remark>

    <exercise>
      <title>f'(0) for n-Factor Product</title>
      <statement><p>If <m>f(x)=(1+x)(1+2x)(1+3x)\cdots(1+nx)</m>, find <m>f'(0)</m>.</p></statement>
      <hint><p>Use the general product rule, and plug in <m>x=0</m>.</p></hint>
      <answer><p><m>f'(0)=1+2+\cdots+n</m>, which is the sum of the first <m>n</m> natural numbers, which is equal to <m>\frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
